00:35:02.539 --> 00:35:04.608
sharpness. So you

00:35:04.609 --> 00:35:06.568
can think of this as a negative feedback loop.

00:35:07.369 --> 00:35:09.709
Yeah. When the sharpness exceeds two over eight, grading

00:35:10.029 --> 00:35:11.739
descent oscillates along the top eigenvector,

00:35:12.299 --> 00:35:13.679
but in turn, those oscillations

00:35:14.599 --> 00:35:16.819
automatically perform gradient descent on the sharpness.

00:35:18.229 --> 00:35:19.938
Thereby allowing gradient descent

00:35:20.259 --> 00:35:22.479
to automatically remain inside the stable region.

00:35:23.379 --> 00:35:25.579
So let me revisit the behavior of gradient descent

00:35:26.828 --> 00:35:29.119
that we saw before. So

00:35:32.389 --> 00:35:34.549
initially, when the sharpness is bigger than two of

00:35:34.549 --> 00:35:36.009
VARTA, that leads to

00:35:36.569 --> 00:35:38.589
exponentially growing oscillations. But in

00:35:38.589 --> 00:35:39.489
turn, the oscillations

00:35:41.099 --> 00:35:43.229
implicitly take negative

00:35:43.229 --> 00:35:45.559
gradient steps on the sharpness pushing

00:35:45.559 --> 00:35:47.618
the sharpness down. These two things are coupled.

00:35:48.179 --> 00:35:50.499
And if you want to analyze gradient descent, you go and analyze

00:35:50.499 --> 00:35:51.098
that coupling.

