you get an equal sign, and then you just have to output

07:22:02.216 --> 07:22:04.286
the answer. The other one is chain of thought,

07:22:04.286 --> 07:22:06.655
which I talked to talked about

07:22:06.655 --> 07:22:08.815
before in the case of parity. So you run you create

07:22:08.815 --> 07:22:10.895
the running answer, and then the last

07:22:10.895 --> 07:22:12.996
element of that will be the the the

07:22:12.996 --> 07:22:15.156
correct final answer. And then we do another one which

07:22:15.156 --> 07:22:17.227
we call aligned chain of thought,

07:22:17.288 --> 07:22:19.368
which works well specifically for RNNs, where

07:22:19.368 --> 07:22:21.756
we also do chain of thought, but the outputs

07:22:22.296 --> 07:22:24.376
are generated along the way as you process

07:22:24.376 --> 07:22:26.226
the sequence. And and,

07:22:27.076 --> 07:22:29.477
we test these types of models

07:22:29.477 --> 07:22:31.416
and and and supervision regimes

07:22:31.986 --> 07:22:34.026
across three scenarios. We train the models

07:22:34.026 --> 07:22:36.221
on a fixed sequence, of a

07:22:36.221 --> 07:22:38.296
fixed length, train them on a whole

07:22:38.296 --> 07:22:40.096
set of lengths between

07:22:40.315 --> 07:22:42.190
two and that target length,

07:22:42.626 --> 07:22:44.325
and we train them in a curriculum

07:22:45.036 --> 07:22:47.241
from short to long sequences. On that

07:22:47.241 --> 07:22:49.262
whole set. Of

07:22:49.262 --> 07:22:51.315
lengths. And then we, so we do that in

07:22:51.315 --> 07:22:53.575
in around 200,000 training

07:22:53.575 --> 07:22:55.966
runs in the end, by doing a binary search

07:22:55.966 --> 07:22:58.424
to find the optimal or

07:22:58.424 --> 07:23:00.646
smallest number of training examples, for

07:23:00.646 --> 07:23:02.887
which each of these models, each of these settings,

